  "rationale": "With no defences, a full attack path exists: phish_workstation, then pivot_internal, then auth_appserver, then kerberoast_dc (stolen_admin), then auth_dc, then escalate_dc, then dump_dbadmin_dc, then auth_db, then escalate_db, then exfiltrate_db. So the answer is at least 1. Deploying mfa:admin alone stops every path. Every exfiltrate technique needs admin_db, admin_fileserver or admin_backup. Those need a foothold from auth_db, auth_fileserver or auth_backup, which all require stolen_dbadmin. The only sources of stolen_dbadmin are dump_dbadmin_dc and dump_dbadmin_adfs, which need admin_dc or admin_adfs. Those in turn need foothold_dc or foothold_adfs. These footholds come only from auth_dc or auth_adfs, and both require NOT mfa_admin. So mfa_admin blocks exfiltration, and the minimum is 1."
